t(1, 2010, """Is it right for a Muslim to show his uniqueness in significant situations? The answer is yes, if a necessity requires it that is clearly connected with setting the earth right, and he alone is expected to do it.

From this angle I will show the refutation of the charge that Imam Ali, peace be upon him, sought the caliphate while the two Shaykhs, may Allah be pleased with them, were present and alive. While al-Siddiq and al-Faruq were alive he did not seek the caliphate, because he was reassured that the competent were alive and provided for; and when death came to al-Faruq, he considered himself definitely the most entitled to the caliphate after them both.

The stance of Imam Ali ibn Abi Talib, peace be upon him, was fully in line with the Companions' understanding, may Allah be pleased with them, of the hadith of Ghadir Khumm. He saw in this hadith a great badge of honour and a special religious standing with which the Prophet ﷺ distinguished him by the command of Allah the Exalted, not out of the Prophet's own love, peace and blessings be upon him and his family. The hadith of Ghadir Khumm turned the Ummah's relationship with Imam Ali from a mere relationship of personal love or hatred, in which a person has latitude toward another, into a divine command to love, ally with and support Imam Ali. Imam Ali would cite the hadith in""")

t(2, 60, """specific situations, not to prove his right to political rule, but to prove his merit and the love of him, which is a divine command, and to repel the harshness of some people who hated him out of jealousy and envy of his standing.
1. The day of "al-Rahbah" in Kufa (the most famous of the situations):
This happened when Imam Ali was caliph of the Muslims; he went out to the people in the square of Kufa (al-Rahbah). There were some dissenters and upstarts who disparaged his merit or doubted his standing because of the fitnas and wars that had occurred (such as the battles of the Camel and Siffin).

What did he do? Imam Ali stood and adjured the Companions present, saying: "I adjure by Allah every Muslim who heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say what he said on the day of Ghadir Khumm to stand."
The result: A large number of Companions stood (it is said a dozen or so men) and testified that they heard the Prophet ﷺ say: "Whoever I am his mawla, Ali is his mawla."
* The purpose of citing it: Imam Ali wanted here to remind the resentful or doubting people that loving and supporting him is a religious obligation commanded by the Messenger ﷺ, and so his descendant will be treated at the end of time, because supporting and obeying him will be obligatory.

2. Citing the hadith in the council of six
After the death of al-Faruq 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, six of the senior Companions were chosen to select the new caliph from among them, and Ali, peace be upon him, was one of them.
* In this council, Imam Ali reminded the other five Companions of his merits and precedence in Islam, to show that he was the most entitled to the caliphate for his merit and knowledge.
* Within a long hadith he said to them: "Is there among you anyone to whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whoever I am his mawla, Ali is his mawla..' other than me?" They all said: "By Allah, no."
* The purpose of citing it: Imam Ali, peace be upon him, used the hadith here as a "merit and distinction" making him more entitled to the matter than others, just as he cited his kinship to the Prophet, his courage and his knowledge.

3. Defending himself against harshness and hatred
In various situations some hypocrites or Kharijites showed hatred toward Imam Ali, and he would remind them of the hadith of Ghadir to establish the proof against them, because hating him contradicts the Prophet's ﷺ explicit command to ally with and love him.

These situations do not prove that Imam Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, violated the Prophet's prohibition of seeking leadership. The scholars of fiqh and Shari'ah distinguish between "the prohibited seeking of leadership" and "clarifying eligibility and competence in councils of shura and election." The Prophet's prohibition in the noble hadith, "O 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, do not ask for leadership," means:
* Passion and inner greed for power.
* A person imposing himself on people without being asked to.

* As for Imam Ali, peace be upon him, he did not go to the people saying "Appoint me over you"; rather it was Caliph 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who chose him and officially appointed him among the six candidates (the shura council). Imam Ali entered the council by a religious and formal order, and when they were asked to choose the most suitable, it was his religious duty to set out his merits and precedence to help them take the right decision, by clarifying to them his eligibility and merit.

2. Following our master Yusuf, peace be upon him:
In the Noble Qur'an we find a similar model, when the Prophet Yusuf, peace be upon him, said to the king of Egypt: "Appoint me over the storehouses of the land; indeed I am a knowing guardian."

* Scholars explain this: if a person finds in himself competence and trustworthiness that may not exist in others, and the community is going through a stage of choice, he may (indeed it may be obligatory for him) mention his qualifications and experience to protect the interests of the Muslims, not out of love of prestige or position, and this is what Imam Ali, peace be upon him, did.

* When Imam Ali mentioned the hadith of Ghadir and his other merits, he was exercising his natural right within the council to set out "the entitlement of merit", and it was not a demand nor jostling over the world, but advice to the council and a reminder of the Prophetic criteria in choosing the suitable and qualified caliph.

His true asceticism in rule
History proves that Imam Ali was not greedy for the seat or for power as such: after the killing of 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, the Muslims came to him hurrying and weeping and asked him to take the caliphate. He refused at first and said to them: "Leave me and seek someone other than me.. I am better for you as a minister than as an emir." He accepted it only after the general public and the Companions insisted on him, fearing the loss of the Ummah and fitna.""")

h(3, 450, """Imam Ali showed his uniqueness after the Prophet, and this involves no self-praise""")

