t(7, 850, """There is a huge difference between the dominance of the situation over the word and the original meaning of the word (its technical sense). The walidayn, "a walid and a walidah" (begetting father and begetting mother), are in the origin of their description wholly good, and this is a fixed principle in the meaning of walidayn as a term and concept. But the dominance of the situation over the word came to set a difference between walidayn and abawayn (father and mother), and events ran in people's lives that revealed differences in behaviour between the word "walidayn" and the word "abawayn". And the Book of our Mighty Lord came, and we find in it a technical distinction. Even so, at the close of my talk today on this issue, about which I put up several earlier posts, I conclude by saying that the problem in the distinction between the word "walidayn" and the word "abawayn" is a distinction of behaviour, not of words. So every father (ab) is definitely a begetter (walid), and every begetter remains only possibly a father.

Zakariyya, peace be upon him, was a father and a begetter, and his wife was a mother and a begetter; they were not merely a begetter and a begetter!

For our Mighty Lord, holy are His names, said, praising His prophet Yahya, peace be upon him:

The verse: "And dutiful to his walidayn, and he was not a disobedient tyrant" (Maryam: 14).

So every prophet is definitely a begetter and a father, not merely a begetter.""")

h(7, 1950, """Clarifying the concept: the word "walidayn" does not mean failure in upbringing is inevitable!""")

t(8, 85, """The Noble Qur'an used the word "my walidah" on the tongue of Isa, peace be upon him, and not "my mother (ummi)", because the situation focuses on the emotional and psychological side and pure kindness; his walidah Maryam, peace be upon her, had not yet begun her care of him so that her name and her description would become "mother".

Unpacking the Qur'anic meaning in the verse
* The father's lineage cut off: Isa, peace be upon him, was born of a mother without a father, so the word "walidah" specifies the side he was born from directly, through the bond of the pure womb and mutual tenderness.
* The axis of emotion and kindness: The verse came in the context of clearing the walidah and showing her tenderness and gentleness ("And dutiful to my walidah, and He did not make me a wretched tyrant"), and kindness (birr) is the summit of emotional and psychological giving.
* Absence of a context of shared responsibility: He did not say "to my mother (ummi)" because the word "umm" in the Qur'an is mostly connected with responsibility, upbringing and legislative rulings (such as inheritance and nursing), while the context here focused on repelling the accusation from her and honouring her emotionally and psychologically before her people.""")

h(8, 950, """Clarifying the concept: why did Allah's Prophet Isa say "and dutiful to my walidah" and not "to my mother"?""")

t(8, 1710, """To the sons and daughters who have walidayn and not fathers and mothers: these words are important and decisive in your lives, worldly and hereafter

Many sons and daughters find a wrong understanding in an imprecise description of their relationship with their walidayn who command them to transgression and aggression on earth, and command them to injustice. Injustice is of two kinds: a person's injustice to himself and his injustice to others. The contexts of conflict between a son and his walidayn or one of them, or between a daughter and her walidayn or one of them, are uncontrolled contexts as regards this son's or this daughter's understanding of how grave the effect is of the command to transgression and injustice from the walidayn or one of them, its effect on the aims of the Shari'ah as a whole and on the five great necessities.

For this reason, my advice to every son and daughter with a problem in their relationship with their walidayn is to seek counsel, and not to decide on their own that the degree of the command from the walidayn to the son or daughter has reached the degree of real, clear and decisive effect on the aims of the Shari'ah and the five great necessities, because in many cases sons and daughters exaggerate in their deduction. And I know the exaggeration happens out of a human impulse of sons and daughters who would like to find any justification or pretext to escape a hell in millions of Muslim homes. But the matter can never proceed soundly this way, and many cases contain real undutifulness by sons and daughters who imagined, with unwise minds not granted wisdom, that their walidayn, both or one of them, had reached with them the degree of dropping the aims of the Shari'ah, while the matter had not reached this certain limit, so the undutifulness of distance and estrangement occurs. So let no one take a decision by himself until he refers to the people of insight, understanding, wisdom and knowledge.

For those who do not know what the five great necessities are, the following information is not my words, for the sake of scholarly honesty.""")

t(9, 60, """--------------------------------
The five great necessities are the basic rights Islam came to protect and preserve for the uprightness of human life, without which the balances of life are disturbed and society collapses. The scholars of usul ranked them by importance as follows:

1. Preserving religion: The greatest and first of the necessities in Islam. It is protected on the side of existence by establishing its pillars such as prayer and tawhid, and on the side of non-existence by answering doubts and preventing innovations.

2. Preserving life: Meaning safeguarding every person's right to life. It is protected on the side of existence by food, drink and medical treatment, and on the side of non-existence by forbidding killing and aggression and legislating retribution as a punishment for criminals.

3. Preserving the mind: Meaning protecting a person's intellectual capacity and the basis of religious accountability. It is protected on the side of existence by seeking knowledge and reflection, and on the side of non-existence by forbidding wine, drugs and everything that removes awareness.

4. Preserving lineage (or honour): Meaning the continuation of human existence, the purity of lineages and the safeguarding of dignity. It is protected on the side of existence by legislating marriage and building the family, and on the side of non-existence by forbidding zina, slander and violation of sanctities.

5. Preserving wealth: Meaning safeguarding riches, property and the lifeblood of the economy. It is protected on the side of existence by legislating trade, work and lawful earning, and on the side of non-existence by forbidding theft, usury and bribery and applying penalties to them.""")

h(9, 1450, """Some children imagined that their fathers seek to make them drop the five great necessities!""")

t(9, 2135, """What you will read now is of greater importance than you imagine! Read to the end

To understand the precise and well-ordered differences between the Qur'anic use of the word "father and mother" and the word "walidayn", we must understand the following:

1. The word "walidayn":
The natural bond and close emotion
* Meaning: Derived from (birth, wiladah); it is the direct and inevitable organic, biological bond that a person cannot change.
* Shari'ah context: This word is always invoked in places of feelings, kindness, prayer, thanks and emotional response, as a reminder of the womb and of human weakness at the start of formation (pregnancy and birth).""")

t(10, 60, """* Purpose: It is not limited to pure emotion; rather it is the emotional drive by which a person performs the hardest duties and responsibilities toward his walidayn, such as bearing the hardships of rejection or harm.

2. The word "abawayn" and "ab (father)":
Care, upbringing and formal responsibility
* Meaning: Derived from (fatherhood, al-ubuwwah); in the language the "ab" is the one who is tender, sets right, raises and takes charge of a thing. So the word widened to include the grandfather, the uncle, the teacher and the leader of the Ummah (e.g. "the religion of your father Ibrahim").
* Shari'ah context: Invoked in places of financial rulings, lineage, inheritance, formal guardianship and doctrinal, intellectual and psychological guidance.
* Purpose: Focusing on the side of responsibility and duty; for Allah the Exalted, speaking of the distribution of wealth, said "and for his abawayn" so the ruling would fit the father (holder of the great financial guardianship and obliged to provide).

3. The particularity of the word "umm (mother)": the nurturing and sacred human vessel
* Meaning: Derived from (al-amm), the origin, reference and pillar of everything (e.g. Umm al-Qura, Umm al-Kitab).
* Shari'ah context: Mentioned on its own for intense veneration and great reverence joined with righteousness, sanctity and inviolability (e.g. "and his wives are their mothers"), and when detailing her sole bearing of the responsibility of bodily suffering which the man is incapable of.

So the Qur'an and Sunnah's use of these words is not random; rather it is a great, deep and complex linguistic engineering that brings out the emotional meaning in the context of moral and heartfelt dealing.

(The walidayn): here there are no responsibilities; whether they did well or did badly in a way that does not certainly drop the aims of the Shari'ah, they definitely have the right of kindness.

As for the other face (the abawayn), it brings out the legal, formal and wise educational meaning in the context of rights, duties, inheritance and good companionship (father - mother), and here we speak of the responsibilities of fatherhood and the responsibilities of motherhood.

The man who betrayed his responsibility and the woman who betrayed her responsibility are a begetter and a begetter, not a father and a mother, and they definitely have the full right to kindness and good treatment, as long as they do not apply continuous, violent and harsh pressure to drop the five great necessities, and kindness to them is always hard and heavy!

The man who did not betray his responsibility and the woman who did not betray her responsibility are a father and a mother; they have the full right to kindness and good treatment, and kindness to them is always easy and beautiful!

But we see children's love for the father and the mother, so kindness comes from intense love .. And we see children's anger at the begetting father and mother, so kindness comes through compulsion, coercion, reluctance and pure obedience to Allah the Exalted; were it not for this obedience and fear of Allah, kindness to the walidayn would not happen. This is a statement and clarification of what I wrote in the post!

And because kindness to the abawayn, not the walidayn, is sweeter to children's hearts than honey and pure water, our Lord did not enjoin kindness to the abawayn, that is, our Lord, glorified and exalted, did not use the word "abawayn", because they do not need an injunction to be made about them at all. And because kindness to the walidayn swings, with lower and higher degrees, between kindness with slight reluctance and kindness with great reluctance, our Mighty Lord enjoined kindness to those who failed to be fathers and mothers to their children (they failed to be abawayn, meaning they remained mere walidayn), because their failure will make kindness to them heavy on the children's souls. Therefore the Lord's injunction and the divine command came concerning the walidayn in word, meaning, concept and kind, for these walidayn failed to win the hearts of their sons and daughters, and the relationship required a clear, decisive and strong divine intervention so that they would receive kindness within the bounds of the Shari'ah, not the kindness of the second Jahiliyyah.""")

h(10, 2300, """100% of the verses of kindness said (walidayn) and did not say abawayn! Come, let us clarify the terms so the Ummah understands!""")

t(11, 595, """And what is the sin of the wronged one if he prayed? O office holders in government departments around the Islamic world: is there anyone who takes heed? Is there anyone sensible? Is there anyone who fears for his fate?!""")

