t(13, 2010, """I do not know whether there is a child in need of help. I ask Allah the Exalted for healing for every sick person, and perhaps the matter is far from the meaning of there being a sick child

Yesterday I saw a dream whose interpretation I have not yet grasped, because I do not interpret with my mind or decode symbols before the meaning falls into my heart, even if it is late, and perhaps it never comes; I wait .. The dream was in a house that is not my house, in which were a father and a child, and the child was sick, sitting in a pram mostly black in colour. Then a noble brother appeared, a shaykh whose channel shows up for me from time to time; I have now learned his name: Mahmud ibn 'Umar. We were standing at the door of the room, and he asked me: "May I get to know you?" I said: "My name is so-and-so and I have a channel." He said: "Beautiful, masha'Allah." Then I went to the sick child and spoke to him; he was between one and two years old perhaps, and it was as if I was treating the child of his illness by talking and speaking with him. Though sick, the child was aware, with some weakness. The room was elegant and grand, with a carpet and a grand mid-sized desk, and its lighting was daylight but without electricity. The child's father did not clearly appear in the dream. End.

I do not talk to myself about anyone as persons, with all respect to everyone of course, but I am occupied with issues that keep me from""")

t(14, 60, """thinking about persons ..

The reason for publishing the dream is my fear that the shaykh may have a sick child on his side who needs ruqyah or something of the sort .. I do not yet know the truth of its interpretation, because I do not interpret with my mind but wait to feel its understanding with my heart, and this requires purifying the thought and the heart of preoccupations and calm of mind. So, contrary to my habit, I published it fearing there is a child in need of help. If the child in the dream truly symbolises a sick child, it bears this meaning even if it bears indirect meanings; with the possibility of the direct meaning of there being a sick child, publishing it is called for, in the hope it reaches our brother; had I published the dream on his side, the chance of his not reading it would be higher than my publishing it here.""")

t(14, 1580, """Our Mighty Lord, the King, exalted be His majesty, said: "And whoever comes to Him a believer having done righteous deeds, for those are the highest ranks" [Ta-Ha: 75].
And our Mighty Lord, holy are His names and attributes, great in the heavens and on earth, exalted be He greatly, said: "And for all there are ranks from what they did" [Al-An'am: 132].
So the difference in ranks came as a result of deeds.
And our Mighty Lord, the King, said: "For whoever of you wills to advance or fall behind" [Al-Muddaththir: 37]""")

h(14, 2200, """The ranks of Paradise are your decision, so do not imagine otherwise!""")

t(15, 440, """From Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: "All my Ummah will enter Paradise except whoever refuses." They said: "O Messenger of Allah, and who refuses?" He said: "Whoever obeys me enters Paradise, and whoever disobeys me has refused.\"""")

h(15, 900, """Entering Paradise or the Fire is your decision, so do not imagine otherwise""")

t(15, 1610, """A true dream, by the grace of Allah the Exalted; I saw it in 2021 on the bed of a deadly illness, not an ordinary illness ..

Enlightened by what I saw, and to spur the resolve, work and patience of every Muslim man and woman, I will devote a recording to the noble hadith, not to the dream, in which I will deal with the work by which, if Allah the Exalted wills, reaching the lofty chambers of Paradise is hoped for, not ordinary Paradise. By the power of Allah the Exalted, our Mighty Lord, I will deal with the saying of our noble Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him and his family: "Men who believed in Allah and believed the messengers".

And around this we will circle, if Allah the Exalted wills.

The agreed-upon hadith
"The people of Paradise will see the people of the chambers above them as you see the brilliant star remaining on the horizon, in the east or the west, because of the difference in rank between them"
They said: "O Messenger of Allah, those are the abodes of the prophets which no others will reach?" He said: "Yes, by Him in whose hand is my soul, men who believed in Allah and believed the messengers"

In the language of the Arabs, al-kawkab al-durri is the very bright star. As for al-ghabir, its meaning is the very far away.""")

h(16, 350, """On the explanation of the hadith of the chambers and its meaning: did I see Paradise in 2021?""")

t(16, 890, """Your disagreement with the post on the 1 percent and the 99 percent was in a crude and arrogant way, so you were blocked. Disagreement must be with courtesy, and this is my reply to your objection to the example in the post on 1% and 99%. I am not arrogant, and I do not accept arrogance in the manner of speaking.

The follower who was blocked objected to my giving an example about Quraysh in the post on 1 and 99%, saying that all of Quraysh disbelieved "in denial against knowledge" (juhud), not because they believed the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him and his family, was lying, and that they all disbelieved, by his claim, while knowing deep inside themselves that he, peace and blessings be upon him and his family, was truthful in the call of Islam, 100% certain. By his claim they all believed the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him and his family, was truthful in everything he called them to, but they rejected Islam in denial while knowing it is the true religion, with not even 1% doubt that it is the true religion!! Your words are a mistake and not correct. The verse you cited about all of Quraysh disbelieving in denial is a verse that deals with "a part of Quraysh", not all of Quraysh as you thought, arrogantly, in an arrogant style. The scholar and the shaykh do not show arrogance when speaking! The street is the place of every possible flaw, but the gatherings of knowledge have their etiquette.

So were verses revealed about Quraysh speaking of some of them who truly thought, deep in themselves and convinced with all their mental faculties, that our noble Prophet was, in their belief, truly a liar?! The answer is yes, and it is not, as you claim, that they all disbelieved only in denial.

Our Mighty Lord, holy are His names and attributes, great in the heavens and on earth, exalted be He greatly, said: "Rather, they have denied that whose knowledge they did not encompass and whose interpretation has not yet come to them" (Yunus: 39).
And our Mighty Lord, the King, exalted be His majesty, said: "Rather, they are in doubt, playing" (Ad-Dukhan: 9). And He, glorified be He, said: "And indeed they are in disquieting doubt about it." And He, exalted, great and holy, said: "They follow nothing but assumption, and they do nothing but guess" (Yunus: 66).

Not all the "chieftains" and leaders in Quraysh were of one man's heart in degree of awareness and knowledge. Although the overwhelming majority of the senior leaders disbelieved with the disbelief of denial and arrogance (that is, they were certain of the truth inwardly and were obstinate outwardly), some of the chieftains disbelieved with a real disbelief of rejection in their hearts, for several structural reasons in their mentality.

The chieftains can accordingly be classed into two groups:
1. Chieftains who disbelieved with the disbelief of "real rejection" in their hearts, minds and mental perception.
These could not mentally grasp the idea of prophethood and resurrection, and believed deep in themselves that the Prophet ﷺ was fabricating or deluded. The most prominent of them:

* Umayyah ibn Khalaf: He was among the most ignorant and coarse of the chieftains, and saw the matter of resurrection after death and the reckoning as mentally and physically impossible. When he brought decayed bones and crumbled them before the Prophet ﷺ, saying: "O Muhammad, do you claim Allah will raise this after it has decayed?!", he did so out of complete inner conviction that Muhammad ﷺ was speaking of impossibilities; so, O arrogant one, his disbelief was the disbelief of rejection, not denial.

* Al-'As ibn Wa'il al-Sahmi (the father of 'Amr ibn al-'As) was a model of the mocker who firmly believed the call of Islam was a lie. When the Prophet's ﷺ son (al-Qasim) died, al-'As went out announcing among Quraysh that Muhammad was "cut off\"""")
